hi there hi this is Steve Kaufman here  the linguist on language blog and I'm  going to ramble again today I'm going to  talk about speaking and listening do we  speak our way to fluency or do we listen  our way to fluency and those of you who  follow this blog know that I'm a great  fan of listening I think the development  of the mp3 player and all the different  devices we have today has just made  listening such a wonderful way to  acquire knowledge and to acquire skills  now many people say they like watching  movies and videos and they like watching  stuff on their iPod which is fine but  I'm more partial to listening today I  was in my car for a good hour going to  different meetings and I was listening  to a history of Sweden in Swedish you  know I learned so much not only is it  good for my Swedish I learn about the  history of Sweden me my car is my  University lecture hall now it is true  that occasionally I tuned out but I also  tune out when I'm in a lecture hall so  maybe I tune out more while driving but  I still pick up a lot of information and  makes me want to go and read more on the  subject it's a two media's phenomenal  and certainly I credit the availability  of modern listening devices like the  iPod or mp3 player and the availability  of all kinds of wonderful stuff on the  internet I download some Portuguese  today on the Portuguese economy I'm  learning about history I'm doing it in  different languages using up dead time I  I would never have learned Russian if I  couldn't have mostly listened my way  through to the level that I have such as  it is so and I believe I very much  believe as I said in previous posts that  that in our efforts to get kids to read  more we should focus more on their  listening skills and we should  deliberately teach and train them in the  ability to listen to things like  mp3 players now I should apologize if I  look like I'm looking down here because  I'm tired of sitting so I'm standing  I've got one of these